{
  "dom_id": "step-q108126",
  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "Given that the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax), & 0<x\\le 3 \\\\ 2x-6, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nis continuous at  $x=3,$ find the value of $a.$",
  "worked_solution": "At $x=3,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}g(x) & =g(3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(2x-6) \\\\  & =2(3)-6 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3a) & =0 \\\\ 3a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
